WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.000 --> 00:00:07.400
We have 32 processes running
simultaneously in user mode.

2
00:00:07.580 --> 00:00:12.480
They have one simple job, register their
process ID in a single shared counter.

3
00:00:12.920 --> 00:00:16.360
If everything executes as intended,
the math is straightforward.

4
00:00:16.680 --> 00:00:20.380
The counter should neatly tick
up and stop at exactly 32.

5
00:00:20.860 --> 00:00:25.390
But looking at this terminal output from
the shm_race program, we see a different

6
00:00:25.440 --> 00:00:31.470
reality. The results are entirely
unpredictable, returning totals like 19, 28, or

7
00:00:31.520 --> 00:00:34.560
25. The programs are not crashing.

8
00:00:34.800 --> 00:00:38.960
Instead, the registration data is
silently disappearing during execution.

9
00:00:39.600 --> 00:00:42.420
These processes are communicating
using shared memory.

10
00:00:42.540 --> 00:00:46.190
They're writing directly to the exact
same space, which, as these missing

11
00:00:46.240 --> 00:00:48.760
registrations prove, is highly dangerous.

12
00:00:49.200 --> 00:00:53.310
To solve the mystery of the vanishing data,
we have to look at how processes bypass

13
00:00:53.360 --> 00:00:56.680
their own isolation to share
memory in the first place.

14
00:00:56.980 --> 00:01:01.650
Normally, the operating system keeps
processes completely isolated, separated from

15
00:01:01.700 --> 00:01:03.540
the kernel by the privilege boundary.

16
00:01:03.780 --> 00:01:08.130
We can pierce that isolation using a
system call named mmap with the MAP_SHARED

17
00:01:08.180 --> 00:01:13.190
flag. This allows two processes to map a
common file, pointing their virtual pages

18
00:01:13.240 --> 00:01:15.500
to the exact same physical frame.

19
00:01:15.720 --> 00:01:20.310
The kernel manages this physical memory
using strict geographic rules, allocating it

20
00:01:20.360 --> 00:01:23.500
in whole pages of exactly 4,096 bytes.

21
00:01:23.740 --> 00:01:27.290
This output from the mmap_limits
program reveals the first rule.

22
00:01:27.340 --> 00:01:31.570
If a process attempts to touch a page that
lies entirely past the end of the file,

23
00:01:31.620 --> 00:01:33.180
there is no data to bring in.

24
00:01:33.240 --> 00:01:35.980
The kernel intervenes and
sends a SIGBUS signal.

25
00:01:36.240 --> 00:01:38.020
There is a second boundary limit.

26
00:01:38.260 --> 00:01:42.370
If a process tries to write to a mapped
area protected with read-only permissions,

27
00:01:42.420 --> 00:01:45.480
the kernel intervenes again
by sending a SIGSEGV signal.

28
00:01:45.900 --> 00:01:50.470
So while mmap successfully creates an open
arena for data, the kernel still acts as

29
00:01:50.520 --> 00:01:53.400
a strict border guard enforcing
boundaries and permissions.

30
00:01:53.560 --> 00:01:56.670
There is another way to share data
that does not require a backing file.

31
00:01:56.720 --> 00:02:02.290
We can use System V shared memory,
calling shmget and shmat to create a

32
00:02:02.340 --> 00:02:06.990
standalone memory block. The defining
characteristic of this System V memory is its

33
00:02:07.040 --> 00:02:11.570
persistence. The segment actually
outlives the processes that created it.

34
00:02:11.620 --> 00:02:16.860
It will sit in the system holding its data
until a process explicitly destroys it.

35
00:02:16.920 --> 00:02:22.180
Whether we map a file or use System V,
we get an incredibly fast memory space.

36
00:02:22.360 --> 00:02:27.840
But this raw unprotected access is exactly
what caused our 32 processes to fail.

37
00:02:28.180 --> 00:02:32.880
Let's bring back our 32 processes and place
them around our new shared memory block.

38
00:02:33.240 --> 00:02:37.370
Because shared memory operates entirely
in user mode, communication never passes

39
00:02:37.420 --> 00:02:41.120
through the kernel. There is no
referee to synchronize their actions.

40
00:02:41.420 --> 00:02:45.460
The failure happens during a very simple
operation, incrementing the counter.

41
00:02:45.660 --> 00:02:48.540
At processor speeds, this
looks like one action.

42
00:02:48.640 --> 00:02:52.210
But if we slow down time, we see it
is actually three distinct steps.

43
00:02:52.260 --> 00:02:55.620
Read the value, add
one, and write it back.

44
00:02:55.940 --> 00:03:00.210
Without a kernel referee, two separate
processes can execute the read step at the

45
00:03:00.260 --> 00:03:01.940
exact same fraction of a second.

46
00:03:02.020 --> 00:03:06.960
They both pull a one, both locally add one,
and write a two back to shared memory.

47
00:03:07.100 --> 00:03:10.400
Two processes worked, but the
counter only went up by one.

48
00:03:10.720 --> 00:03:13.340
This mechanical collision
is a race condition.

49
00:03:13.560 --> 00:03:17.410
It is the exact reason our process
registrations silently overrode each other and

50
00:03:17.460 --> 00:03:21.210
vanished. Shared memory gives
us unparalleled raw speed.

51
00:03:21.260 --> 00:03:25.490
But to use it safely, we are forced to
build our own synchronization, which is the

52
00:03:25.540 --> 00:03:28.060
challenge of Unit 7. Let's review.

53
00:03:28.380 --> 00:03:32.870
If a process writes to a page that lies
entirely past the end of a mapped file, what

54
00:03:32.920 --> 00:03:34.420
signal does the kernel send?

55
00:03:34.820 --> 00:03:39.930
SIGBUS. Next. Why does System V shared
memory remain in the system after a program

56
00:03:39.980 --> 00:03:44.670
ends? Because the segment outlives the
processes until someone explicitly destroys

57
00:03:44.720 --> 00:03:51.920
it. Now open animations A6.2 and A6.3
in the course hub, and run lab TD5.
